course: history
lesson: 21
title: Castles, knights and cathedrals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In medieval Europe land was power: the king gave land to lords, the lords promised him soldiers, and peasants farmed the lords' land in return for protection.
explain: The lords lived in castles, first wooden forts on a mound and later stone giants with thick walls, towers, moats and drawbridges, but inside they were cold, smoky and busy, not fairy-tale palaces.
explain: Their best fighters were knights, rich horsemen trained from boyhood, who wore heavy armour and painted coats of arms on their shields so friends could tell who was who in battle.
explain: Picture a castle in a cartoon under siege: in real life the attackers more often just waited for weeks until the defenders ran out of food, and from the 1400s cannons began to smash even the thickest walls.
explain: Meanwhile whole towns spent a hundred years or more building cathedrals, whose stained-glass windows told Bible stories to people who could not read.
fact: In the medieval system often called feudalism, the king gave land to lords, lords owed him soldiers, and peasants owed the lords work and part of their crops.
fact: Most people were peasants who worked from sunrise to sunset, lived in small one-room houses and often kept their animals inside in winter for warmth and safety.
fact: The first castles were wooden towers on earth mounds; later castles were built of stone, with thick walls, towers, moats and drawbridges.
fact: Attackers besieged castles for weeks or months with catapults and trebuchets, or tried to starve the defenders out, and cannons from the 1400s made castle walls far less useful.
fact: A noble boy became a page at about seven, a squire at about fourteen, and could be knighted at about twenty-one.
fact: Early knights wore chain mail and later knights wore fitted plate armour, which was so well made that a knight could run and climb onto his horse by himself.
fact: The story that knights had to be lifted onto horses by cranes is a myth.
fact: Chivalry was the code knights were meant to follow: be brave and loyal, protect the weak and honour God, though real knights often fell short of it.
fact: King Arthur and the Knights of the Round Table are legends, not proven history.
fact: At tournaments knights jousted, charging at each other on horseback with blunted lances in front of cheering crowds.
fact: Coats of arms let knights be recognised when helmets hid their faces, and towns, schools and football clubs still use them.
fact: Cathedrals were huge and built by hand, and money came in slowly, so many took a hundred years or more to finish.
fact: Lincoln Cathedral in England was probably the tallest building in the world for about 200 years, until its spire fell in 1548.
fact: From 1096 European Christians fought the Crusades to control Jerusalem, a city holy to Christians, Jews and Muslims, and Muslim leaders such as Saladin fought back and retook it in 1187.
fact: Both sides in the Crusades did brave things and cruel things, and ordinary people of all three faiths suffered.
exercise: In the feudal system, what did a lord owe the king in return for land? | Soldiers to fight for him | Nothing at all | Gold from his own mines | A new castle every year
exercise: What did peasants owe their lord? | Work on his land and part of their crops | Only a short prayer for him once a year at Easter | Their swords and horses | Nothing, they were free and paid no one
exercise: Why did many peasants keep animals inside the house in winter? | For warmth and to keep them safe | Because animals were holy | Because the law forced them to | So the animals could guard the castle
exercise: What were the very first castles usually made of? | Wood on top of an earth mound | Marble brought by ship from Italy | Ice and snow | Glass and iron
exercise: Which of these made a castle hard to attack? | A moat, thick walls and a drawbridge | A big front garden full of flowers | Large glass windows | A flat roof with no towers at all to climb
exercise: What was the most common way to take a strong castle? | Surround it and starve the defenders out | Ask the lord very politely to pack up and leave | Climb over in one night | Dig it up and move it
exercise: Why did cannons make castles less useful? | Cannonballs could smash even thick stone walls | Cannons were far too heavy to be fired at all by anyone | Castles were built only of wood | Cannons only worked in summer
exercise: What were the steps to becoming a knight? | Page, then squire, then knight | Squire, then king, then knight | Peasant, then monk, then knight | Knight first, then page, then squire
exercise: How did a knight in plate armour get onto his horse? | He climbed on by himself | He was lifted on by a crane | He had to be thrown up by four men | He never rode, he only walked
exercise: What was chivalry? | The code knights were meant to follow | A type of long medieval sword | The name of the highest castle tower | A tax paid by peasants
exercise: What is jousting? | Charging at another knight on horseback with a lance | Fighting on foot with bare fists in a circle of mud and straw | Racing horses without riders | Throwing stones at a castle wall
exercise: Why did knights need coats of arms? | To be recognised when helmets hid their faces | To show how old and how rich they were | To keep the rain off their shiny armour | To scare away horses
exercise: Why did cathedrals have stained-glass windows with Bible stories? | Most people could not read, so pictures told the stories | Glass was cheaper than stone | To keep the building warm in the long winters | So nobody could see inside
exercise: Why was Jerusalem important to so many people? | It is holy to Christians, Jews and Muslims | It was the biggest and busiest port in Europe | It was the only city with a castle | It had the best farmland in the whole world
exercise: When did the First Crusade begin? | In 1096 | In 1492 | In 1066 | In 1914
exercise: Who was Saladin? | A Muslim leader who retook Jerusalem | A French knight of King Arthur's Round Table | An English king | A Viking chief
test: my brother says knights were so heavy they got put on horses with a crane lol | No, a knight in plate armour could climb on by himself | Yes, every knight needed a crane | Yes, but only on Sundays | No, knights never rode horses
test: Ella says King Arthur was a real king of England in 1300. Right? | No, Arthur is a legend, not proven history | Yes, he ruled England for fifty years | Yes, he built Lincoln Cathedral with his knights | No, he was a French knight
test: In a film the Crusaders are all heroes and the other side all villains. What is fairer? | Both sides did brave things and cruel things | The film is exactly right about everything | The Muslims were always the only heroes in every battle | Nobody actually fought in the Crusades
test: A lord's castle has plenty of water and food for a year. What would attackers most likely find? | A siege would take a very long time | The castle would fall in one day | The attackers would win at once | The castle would have to surrender at night
test: your castle in the game's got stone walls, its 1450 and the enemy's got cannons. you safe? | Not really, cannons can break even stone walls | Totally safe, stone never breaks | Safe, cannons were only ever used on ships at sea | Yes, cannons were invented in 1900
test: What would a medieval castle really feel like inside? | Cold, smoky and busy | Warm, quiet and bright like a palace | Empty, nobody lived there | Full of electric lights
test: A boy of seven is sent to serve in a lord's household to learn manners. What is he now? | A page | A squire | A knight | A great lord
test: Why do football clubs and towns have badges with shields and animals today? | They come from the coats of arms of the Middle Ages | Because footballers wear armour | Because kings still order it | They were invented in 2000
test: mate reckons peasants were lazy and just sat about. true or rubbish? | Rubbish, they worked from sunrise to sunset | True, they never worked a day in their lives | True, the knights did the farming | Rubbish, they all lived in castles
test: Why might a town spend over a hundred years building one cathedral? | It was huge, built by hand, and money came slowly | Builders were paid only once a century | Stone could only be cut in the middle of winter | The plans were lost every year in fires
test: In Europa Universalis your army is mostly knights in 1337. What did real knights need that made them rare? | Lots of money for horses and armour | A university degree | Permission from the Pope each day | A cannon of their own
exam: Identify the obligation customarily owed by a lord to the sovereign in exchange for a grant of land under the feudal arrangement. | The provision of soldiers | The payment of all peasants' wages | The construction of cathedrals | The surrender of his coat of arms
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mounting of a horse by a knight clad in plate armour? | He was capable of mounting unaided | He was invariably hoisted by a crane | He was carried by his squire upon his back | Mounting was impossible and he fought on foot
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the city of Jerusalem in the period of the Crusades. | It was held sacred by Christians, Jews and Muslims alike | It was held sacred by Christians alone | It was of no religious consequence | It was the capital of England
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which castle fortifications declined in military utility from the fifteenth century onwards. | The advent of cannon capable of breaching stone walls | The prohibition of castles by the Church throughout Christendom | The disappearance of moats through drought | The abolition of knighthood
